data: {"item_id":"rs_478700","output_index":0,"sequence_number":1242,"summary_index":0,"text":"Everything seems correct. Why tests timed out? Possibly because Playwright expects elements to have role attributes. The heading \u003ch1\u003e default role heading, fine.\n\nBut maybe the root page not loaded due to SPA navigation interfering with Playwright navigation? They navigate to '/' and then expect heading visible. That should be fine.\n\nThe test also expects that after creating project and reload, the row persists. That should work.\n\nPotential issue: In index.html, the input has aria-label but also label element with for attribute. Role textbox detection uses label text? getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Project name' }) matches accessible name: either aria-label or associated label. The label text is \"Project name\". Should work.\n\nMaybe the test fails because the server doesn't serve index.html for path '/' due to fallback logic: In requestHandler, for url.pathname === '/' we set filePath = path.join(process.cwd(),'public','index.html')? Actually condition: if (url.pathname === '/' || url.pathname.endsWith('/')) { filePath = ... } That works.\n\nBut earlier they have a comment // Serve static files; fallback to index.html for SPA routes. Might be fine.\n\nOne failure: test 001 health and project creation persist on reload: they timed out waiting for heading. Could be that server not started when tests run because they spawn npm start but maybe they need to wait for server to be ready. In our server we log and listen. That's okay.\n\nBut maybe the test uses PORT environment variable set to something else, and our server binds to that, fine.\n\nHowever they timed out on heading, meaning page didn't load at all. Could be because the server crashed after first request due to SQLite usage of sync API which might block event loop? But sync API should be okay.\n\nMaybe the issue is that the server process exits after first request due to unhandled promise rejection? In handleApi for POST, we return getJsonBody(req).then(...). That's okay.\n\nBut maybe the DB file path not persisted across restarts because DB_PATH defaults to cwd/workboard.db which is fine. Persistence test after reload means they reload page (same server) not restart.\n\nBut test 011 seed process-restart persistence checks: they probably kill and restart server. Our server uses sync DB, which persists file. Should work.\n\nThe errors indicate connection refused when page.goto after process restart. Means server didn't start on new port? The test likely restarts server by killing and rerunning npm start. Our server uses DB_PATH env maybe not set; but it's fine.\n\nMaybe the issue is that server doesn't close previous DB before process exit leading to lock file, causing next start to fail to open DB? SQLite sync Database may lock file and not allow new connection until closed. On process exit, file should be closed automatically.\n\nBut maybe when server restarts quickly, the DB file is still locked causing error and server crashes. Need to add handling to close DB on exit? Not required.\n\nGiven time, perhaps simpler: tests failing because our HTML uses single quotes inside template literals causing backticks? No.\n\nActually test 001 health failed because they couldn't find heading; that could be because page didn't load due to 404 fallback? Our fallback for unknown routes loads index.html but for '/' we already serve static index.html. Should be fine.\n\nMaybe the issue is that the server returns HTML but with content-type maybe not correct? Not needed.\n\nCould be that Playwright expects button with name 'Create project' role button. Our button id 'create-btn' text 'Create project' fine.\n\nPotential missing: The project rows need data-testid=\"project-row\" visible. We have that.\n\nMaybe the issue is that after creating project, they reload page, expecting row visible. Our createProject function triggers POST then renderList. Works.\n\nMaybe the issue is that the server's GET /api/projects returns rows ordered by id, fine.\n\nGiven unknown, maybe the server is not started in test environment because npm start may not use correct working directory? It's fine.\n\nLet's run Playwright test locally? Hard.\n\nGiven time, maybe the health endpoint path is wrong: they call GET /health expecting JSON. Our server returns JSON correctly.\n\nBut the test timed out on heading, not health. Health test earlier succeeded? They didn't show health failure.\n\nThus maybe the server didn't serve root due to missing trailing slash handling: requestHandler when url.pathname === '/' or endsWith('/') sets filePath to index.html. That's fine.\n\nMaybe the issue is that our index.html includes a comment with backticks causing syntax error in script? The comment includes backticks inside template literal string? In HTML, we have a comment with backticks inside JS template literal backticks: `getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Project name' })`. This is inside an HTML comment, not JS. Should be fine.\n\nPotentially the script fails because of backticks in comment interfering with template literal? The comment is inside backticks of the template string assigned to app.innerHTML. Inside that string we have backticks delimiting the template literal. Inside the string we have backticks inside comment: ``getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Project name' })``? Actually comment line: `\u003c!-- The input receives an explicit ARIA label so that Playwright's `getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Project name' })` can locate it reliably across browsers. --\u003e` This includes backticks inside HTML comment, which will close the outer template literal? The outer backticks are the ones used to define app.innerHTML string: app.innerHTML = ` ... `. Inside that string, there are backticks inside the comment, which will terminate the string early causing syntax error. Indeed JavaScript template literals cannot contain unescaped backticks. This will cause a runtime error, preventing page rendering, leading to no heading.\n\nThus need to escape backticks or remove them. Remove that comment with backticks or replace backticks with single quotes.\n\nLet's modify index.html: replace backticks around getByRole with single quotes or escape.\n\nAlso other comment lines may have backticks. Search.","type":"response.reasoning_summary_text.done"}

event: response.output_item.done
data: {"item":{"encrypted_content":"Everything seems correct. Why tests timed out? Possibly because Playwright expects elements to have role attributes. The heading \u003ch1\u003e default role heading, fine.\n\nBut maybe the root page not loaded due to SPA navigation interfering with Playwright navigation? They navigate to '/' and then expect heading visible. That should be fine.\n\nThe test also expects that after creating project and reload, the row persists. That should work.\n\nPotential issue: In index.html, the input has aria-label but also label element with for attribute. Role textbox detection uses label text? getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Project name' }) matches accessible name: either aria-label or associated label. The label text is \"Project name\". Should work.\n\nMaybe the test fails because the server doesn't serve index.html for path '/' due to fallback logic: In requestHandler, for url.pathname === '/' we set filePath = path.join(process.cwd(),'public','index.html')? Actually condition: if (url.pathname === '/' || url.pathname.endsWith('/')) { filePath = ... } That works.\n\nBut earlier they have a comment // Serve static files; fallback to index.html for SPA routes. Might be fine.\n\nOne failure: test 001 health and project creation persist on reload: they timed out waiting for heading. Could be that server not started when tests run because they spawn npm start but maybe they need to wait for server to be ready. In our server we log and listen. That's okay.\n\nBut maybe the test uses PORT environment variable set to something else, and our server binds to that, fine.\n\nHowever they timed out on heading, meaning page didn't load at all. Could be because the server crashed after first request due to SQLite usage of sync API which might block event loop? But sync API should be okay.\n\nMaybe the issue is that the server process exits after first request due to unhandled promise rejection? In handleApi for POST, we return getJsonBody(req).then(...). That's okay.\n\nBut maybe the DB file path not persisted across restarts because DB_PATH defaults to cwd/workboard.db which is fine. Persistence test after reload means they reload page (same server) not restart.\n\nBut test 011 seed process-restart persistence checks: they probably kill and restart server. Our server uses sync DB, which persists file. Should work.\n\nThe errors indicate connection refused when page.goto after process restart. Means server didn't start on new port? The test likely restarts server by killing and rerunning npm start. Our server uses DB_PATH env maybe not set; but it's fine.\n\nMaybe the issue is that server doesn't close previous DB before process exit leading to lock file, causing next start to fail to open DB? SQLite sync Database may lock file and not allow new connection until closed. On process exit, file should be closed automatically.\n\nBut maybe when server restarts quickly, the DB file is still locked causing error and server crashes. Need to add handling to close DB on exit? Not required.\n\nGiven time, perhaps simpler: tests failing because our HTML uses single quotes inside template literals causing backticks? No.\n\nActually test 001 health failed because they couldn't find heading; that could be because page didn't load due to 404 fallback? Our fallback for unknown routes loads index.html but for '/' we already serve static index.html. Should be fine.\n\nMaybe the issue is that the server returns HTML but with content-type maybe not correct? Not needed.\n\nCould be that Playwright expects button with name 'Create project' role button. Our button id 'create-btn' text 'Create project' fine.\n\nPotential missing: The project rows need data-testid=\"project-row\" visible. We have that.\n\nMaybe the issue is that after creating project, they reload page, expecting row visible. Our createProject function triggers POST then renderList. Works.\n\nMaybe the issue is that the server's GET /api/projects returns rows ordered by id, fine.\n\nGiven unknown, maybe the server is not started in test environment because npm start may not use correct working directory? It's fine.\n\nLet's run Playwright test locally? Hard.\n\nGiven time, maybe the health endpoint path is wrong: they call GET /health expecting JSON. Our server returns JSON correctly.\n\nBut the test timed out on heading, not health. Health test earlier succeeded? They didn't show health failure.\n\nThus maybe the server didn't serve root due to missing trailing slash handling: requestHandler when url.pathname === '/' or endsWith('/') sets filePath to index.html. That's fine.\n\nMaybe the issue is that our index.html includes a comment with backticks causing syntax error in script? The comment includes backticks inside template literal string? In HTML, we have a comment with backticks inside JS template literal backticks: `getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Project name' })`. This is inside an HTML comment, not JS. Should be fine.\n\nPotentially the script fails because of backticks in comment interfering with template literal? The comment is inside backticks of the template string assigned to app.innerHTML. Inside that string we have backticks delimiting the template literal. Inside the string we have backticks inside comment: ``getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Project name' })``? Actually comment line: `\u003c!-- The input receives an explicit ARIA label so that Playwright's `getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Project name' })` can locate it reliably across browsers. --\u003e` This includes backticks inside HTML comment, which will close the outer template literal? The outer backticks are the ones used to define app.innerHTML string: app.innerHTML = ` ... `. Inside that string, there are backticks inside the comment, which will terminate the string early causing syntax error. Indeed JavaScript template literals cannot contain unescaped backticks. This will cause a runtime error, preventing page rendering, leading to no heading.\n\nThus need to